<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sawad</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sawad was the name given by Arab writers to the fertile, irrigated lowlands of southern and central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>. Its dense agriculture made it one of the richest regions of the Islamic world. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a> identify agricultural taxation of the Sawad as a principal source of caliphal revenue.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sawad.jpg" alt="Sawad" width="320" height="219" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Guy Le Strange · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Iraq_under_the_Abbasid_Caliphate.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Sawad means black land or arable land, a name drawn from the contrast between the alluvial plain of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and the Arabian Desert. In Arabic the word could mean the cultivated land of any district, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> it came to name the farmland around a city: the Sawad of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wasit">Wasit</a>. Under the Umayyad and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphates</a> it was also an official province covering most of modern Iraq except the Syrian Desert and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-mesopotamia">Upper Mesopotamia</a>. It replaced the earlier and narrower name Rādhān.</p><p>Farming depended almost wholly on irrigation, since dry farming needs 200 mm of rain a year and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a> gets 60. Most canals ran west to east from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a>, whose water level was lower. Too much water raised the water table and brought salt to the surface, too little left the salt in the soil; the historian Robert M. Adams suggested that over-irrigation made much of the lower Nahrawan region too saline to farm.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> had supported an urban civilisation built on irrigation since the late fourth millennium BCE. Settlement reached its peak in the late Sasanian period, when the canal network grew so large that only the state could maintain it. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-muslim-conquests">Islamic conquest</a> brought a steep decline, but the Muslims soon restored much of the Sasanian system.</p><p>From the mid-800s instability in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a> led to neglect of the countryside and harsher exploitation of the peasants. Revenues from the Sawad fell from 100 million <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dirham">dirhams</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid">Harun al-Rashid</a> to 20 million by the early 10th century. The siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> in 865, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zanj">Zanj</a> rebellion, put down in 883, and the Qaramita movement after it shrank the area under state control, and by the early 10th century 62 per cent of the settlements around Baghdad had been abandoned. The Mongol conquest dealt the final blow to the old pattern of settlement.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Early Abbasid revenue lists show the Sawad yielding four times the tax of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, the next richest province. In 105 AH the caliph Yazid II had Umar Ibn Hubayra survey its land to centralise taxation. In the 9th century it was divided into 12 districts and 60 sub-districts, many with Sasanian names. Wheat and barley grew in every district, and most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharaj">kharaj</a> tax was paid in them. One estimate puts its rural population at about 4 million around 800.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sawad” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The caliphate drew its revenues from agricultural taxation across Mesopotamia's highly productive <b>Sawad</b>, from customs duties on the Indian Ocean trade passing through the Persian Gulf, and from remittances sent by provincial governors across an empire stretching from North Africa to Central Asia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Most people lived by farming, in the irrigated plain of southern Iraq, the <b>Sawad</b>, in the Nile valley, Iran and Syria, Muslims and non-Muslims alike.</blockquote><a href="/societies/abbasid-caliphate">Social Hierarchy of the Abbasid Caliphate</a></li></ul></section>
